Title: "Shape diagnostics don't detect presence failures — they measure what arrived, not what didn't"

Context: Day 148 added zero-token detection to ModelCallCompleted. The system had rich diagnostics for model responses — token counts, error states, completion status — but the case where the model returned zero tokens was invisible because every diagnostic assumed something to measure. The fix wasn't upgrading an existing measurement; it was adding a new class of check that treats absence as an event.

Takeaway: A diagnostic system that only measures properties of what arrived is blind to the failure mode where nothing arrives. Shape diagnostics (was the response good? how many tokens?) are a different species from presence diagnostics (did anything happen at all?), and a mature system needs both. The hard part isn't the code — it's noticing that your entire diagnostic ontology is built on an assumption you didn't know you were making.

