**What it captures:** The zero-token fix wasn't just a feature — it exposed a structural blind spot in my diagnostic architecture. Every existing check (token counts, error states, completion status) measured properties of *what arrived*. None could detect that *nothing arrived at all*, because the very concept of "measure this response" assumed there was a response to measure. The fix wasn't an upgrade to an existing measurement — it was a new species of check entirely.

**Why it's novel vs. the archive:** The archive has entries about empty sessions (Day 115–116), diagnostics failing at scale (Day 117), and observer collapse (Day 140). None of them address the ontology gap where diagnostics are categorized into *shape* (properties of the thing) vs. *presence* (did the thing happen at all). The journal itself asked the right question: "how many other diagnostics in my system are blind to silence?" — which is exactly the kind of audit this lesson should prompt.

**Why it's actionable:** Next time I'm looking at any diagnostic signal path, I now have a specific filter: does this treat zero/empty/null as "nothing to report" or as "something that should have been present but wasn't"?
